Rym: Was it '94?

383
00:30:54,981 --> 00:30:55,946
Rym: I don't know if Wikipedia opened for that.

384
00:30:55,986 --> 00:30:56,971
Rym: I think it came out in 1994, is my guess.

385
00:31:00,420 --> 00:31:01,426
Rym: Anime.

386
00:31:02,572 --> 00:31:03,356
Rym: List of Ranma 1/2 episodes.

387
00:31:05,340 --> 00:31:05,501
Rym: No, 1989.

388
00:31:05,501 --> 00:31:05,942
Rym: '94 was the end.

389
00:31:05,962 --> 00:31:06,545
Rym: '92 was the end.

390
00:31:06,565 --> 00:31:07,509
Rym: That was way off.

391
00:31:11,142 --> 00:31:11,228
Rym: Okay.

392
00:31:11,721 --> 00:31:13,086
Rym: Yeah, '89, the anime started.

393
00:31:13,547 --> 00:31:15,514
Rym: And there are seven seasons of this.

394
00:31:15,995 --> 00:31:17,400
Rym: Seven full seasons.

395
00:31:18,140 --> 00:31:21,280
Scott: There's also OVAs in movies and such, too, right?

396
00:31:21,760 --> 00:31:24,735
Rym: But on one hand, there's a story.

397
00:31:24,997 --> 00:31:25,620
Rym: There's a narrative.

398
00:31:26,020 --> 00:31:40,420
Rym: But on the other hand, a lot of anime in that era, especially stuff, the Rumiko Takahashi stuff, would sort of have a plot in the beginning that really just went until a critical mass of characters had been introduced to you.

399
00:31:41,042 --> 00:31:57,299
Rym: And then it would shift from sort of following the plot to basically just being a sitcom, because they can assume you know these characters, and characters would evolve, and the world would evolve, but there was no longer an overarching plot so much as just, "What bullshit is Ranma on about

400
00:31:57,339 --> 00:31:57,560
Rym: today?".

401
00:31:58,361 --> 00:32:09,200
Scott: Right, because the plot wasn't so much a... Normally when you have a plot and a story that's going to end, you'll create this conflict that needs a resolution, right?

402
00:32:09,240 --> 00:32:11,030
Scott: Like, "The bad guy's gonna take over the world.

403
00:32:11,151 --> 00:32:12,257
Scott: Are you gonna stop

404
00:32:12,317 --> 00:32:12,458
Scott: him?"

405
00:32:13,162 --> 00:32:15,980
Scott: Or something like... That's the most basic one, obviously.

406
00:32:16,701 --> 00:32:35,559
Scott: But the plot for Ranma doesn't really... It's like, "Yeah, okay, so there's this dude and his dad, and they get cursed, and so then they go to this dojo where the dad's friend is in charge, and then they decide to engage their children to each

407
00:32:35,659 --> 00:32:35,860
Scott: other.".

408
00:32:36,040 --> 00:32:38,039
Rym: And the children aren't into it.

409
00:32:39,200 --> 00:32:39,723
Scott: Obviously.

410
00:32:39,743 --> 00:32:40,971
Scott: Who would be?

411
00:32:41,051 --> 00:32:42,580
Scott: Some stranger you've never seen before.

412
00:32:43,300 --> 00:32:52,679
Scott: And the curse allows for hijinks, because the dad turns into a panda, Ranma turns into a female Ranma, and the other dude turns into a pig.

413
00:32:55,322 --> 00:33:01,540
Rym: And from there, there is sort of an early plot around navigating that, and are they really gonna get married?

414
00:33:01,860 --> 00:33:02,763
Rym: How are they gonna get out of this?

415
00:33:03,085 --> 00:33:06,256
Rym: And then it just kind of resolves into, "Well, they're all friends

416
00:33:06,337 --> 00:33:06,558
Rym: now.".

417
00:33:07,641 --> 00:33:14,200
Scott: Yeah, it's like, they could have kept driving the plot to be like, "Okay, are they gonna attempt to eliminate the curse?

418
00:33:14,340 --> 00:33:17,798
Scott: Are they gonna decide to get married or not get

419
00:33:17,858 --> 00:33:18,180
Scott: married?".

420
00:33:18,260 --> 00:33:22,380
Scott: And they could keep droning on about that, but it's like, they don't.

421
00:33:22,760 --> 00:33:24,926
Scott: They're just like, "Well, I'm cursed now.

422
00:33:24,946 --> 00:33:26,510
Scott: That's just the setup.

423
00:33:27,152 --> 00:33:28,114
Scott: We're engaged now.

424
00:33:28,195 --> 00:33:28,977
Scott: That's just the

425
00:33:28,997 --> 00:33:29,338
Scott: setup.".

426
00:33:30,501 --> 00:33:36,860
Scott: And then they just live there and go about their everyday lives in a sitcom Simpsons-like fashion.

427
00:33:38,522 --> 00:33:38,583
Rym: Yup.

428
00:33:39,845 --> 00:33:46,140
Rym: So, every classic anime from the '90s is getting remade, kind of one by one.

429
00:33:46,760 --> 00:33:48,711
Scott: Well, the Rumigotaka Aichi specifically, right?

430
00:33:48,731 --> 00:33:50,220
Scott: They did the Urusayatsura already.

431
00:33:50,440 --> 00:33:51,587
Scott: I don't know if it's still going.

432
00:33:51,607 --> 00:33:53,640
Scott: They're doing the Ranma.

433
00:33:54,040 --> 00:33:59,800
Scott: You have to assume they're gonna do the Inuyasha or the Meisani Goku or something next.

434
00:33:59,920 --> 00:34:02,051
Rym: Call me if... Let's see.

435
00:34:02,353 --> 00:34:03,840
Rym: What would actually get me excited?

436
00:34:04,340 --> 00:34:08,639
Rym: Call me if Escaflowne or Utena get the remake treatment.

437
00:34:10,081 --> 00:34:11,985
Scott: There was recent buzz.

438
00:34:12,809 --> 00:34:16,719
Scott: No one knows why, but there was recent buzz of Escaflowne in Japan recently.

439
00:34:17,042 --> 00:34:18,659
Rym: Not only that, but so weirdly...

440
00:34:18,679 --> 00:34:22,571
Scott: For no particular reason at all that anyone can tell, there was social media.

441
00:34:22,610 --> 00:34:24,476
Scott: People were just like, "Escaflowne!

442
00:34:24,657 --> 00:34:25,319
Scott: Oh, what a good

443
00:34:25,339 --> 00:34:25,560
Scott: show.".

444
00:34:25,699 --> 00:34:37,679
Rym: A couple weeks before I left for London, I actually had a weird random conversation with a co-worker because he knows that I'm an anime person, even though almost everyone watches anime now in this company.

445
00:34:38,481 --> 00:34:41,969
Rym: All my adult co-worker people, they all watch some anime.

446
00:34:42,029 --> 00:34:46,860
Rym: Anime is just a normal thing alongside anything else, but they know that I know a lot about it.

447
00:34:47,601 --> 00:34:53,940
Rym: So, he asked me, "My daughter was talking about this show, and I never heard of it, and I don't know what the deal was.

448
00:34:54,860 --> 00:34:56,092
Rym: Escaflowne, have you ever heard of this

449
00:34:56,132 --> 00:34:56,353
Rym: thing?".

450
00:34:57,221 --> 00:34:59,828
Rym: And I was just like, "How old's your daughter?

451
00:35:00,470 --> 00:35:03,659
Rym: That's not a show that people younger than me tend to know

452
00:35:03,679 --> 00:35:03,879
Rym: about.".

453
00:35:07,723 --> 00:35:15,800
Rym: So now there's a remake, and I would say this remake is not... Because there's two ways you can go with a remake of an anime that itself is based on the manga.

454
00:35:16,241 --> 00:35:24,560
Rym: You can take the anime and redo it, or you could take the manga and make a new anime from the manga.

455
00:35:25,020 --> 00:35:33,619
Rym: And this is more to the latter than the former, but that's not saying much because the original anime was pretty close to the manga to begin with.

456
00:35:35,161 --> 00:35:38,453
Rym: But it has a lot of stylistic elements from the manga.

457
00:35:39,195 --> 00:35:40,500
Rym: The pacing is a little bit different.

458
00:35:40,781 --> 00:35:47,399
Rym: It does a lot of the two-tone backgrounds and manga sound effects stuff that the original anime didn't really do.

459
00:35:48,220 --> 00:35:49,499
Rym: But otherwise, it's very faithful.

460
00:35:50,620 --> 00:35:56,200
Rym: But I am actually enjoying this anime more than I enjoyed the original Aranma.

461
00:35:56,941 --> 00:35:59,260
Rym: And I watched a little bit of the original Aranma again, to be sure.

462
00:35:59,741 --> 00:36:02,159
Rym: And honestly, that show was good for its time.

463
00:36:03,180 --> 00:36:04,125
Rym: The pacing's not great.

464
00:36:04,888 --> 00:36:07,159
Rym: The animation gets pretty janky kind of often.

465
00:36:08,520 --> 00:36:10,129
Scott: You're cranking out that many episodes.

466
00:36:10,792 --> 00:36:12,220
Scott: They spent a lot of money on them.

467
00:36:12,520 --> 00:36:18,427
Rym: And I'd say that today, if someone's never seen this show and you're interested in it... Like, one, just read the manga.

468
00:36:18,628 --> 00:36:20,200
Rym: The manga is still a good way to experience this.

469
00:36:20,380 --> 00:36:22,247
Rym: But I just watched the new one.

470
00:36:22,488 --> 00:36:24,436
Rym: I wouldn't really start with the old one.

471
00:36:24,496 --> 00:36:25,440
Rym: I would just watch the new one.

472
00:36:26,240 --> 00:36:31,500
Scott: Yeah, I can't really compare the old and the new because, like I said, for the old, I only saw a smattering of episodes.

473
00:36:31,700 --> 00:36:34,240
Scott: I actually never saw it in order or anything, right?

474
00:36:34,300 --> 00:36:39,479
Scott: So I just knew... I actually didn't know that it was because of some curse until I watched the new episodes.

475
00:36:39,960 --> 00:36:42,046
Scott: I thought it was just like, "That's how it is.

476
00:36:42,107 --> 00:36:42,708
Scott: That's the thing.

477
00:36:42,789 --> 00:36:46,039
Scott: The show's about this guy who gets wet and turns into a

478
00:36:46,079 --> 00:36:46,279
Scott: girl.".

479
00:36:46,641 --> 00:36:51,800
Rym: Well, because you got all these other characters who have fallen in or eventually fall into a spring.

480
00:36:53,061 --> 00:36:54,625
Scott: Yep.

481
00:36:54,646 --> 00:36:58,658
Scott: It's like, I didn't know how it got that way or if it ever doesn't be that way.

482
00:36:58,698 --> 00:36:59,340
Scott: I didn't know nothing.

483
00:37:00,342 --> 00:37:02,880
Scott: Because I'd only seen, you know, whatever.

484
00:37:03,762 --> 00:37:20,840
Scott: But what I can say about the new show is that there is at least one excellent artistic element of the new show that really stands out, is that at certain moments, they're actually changing the visual style of the show intentionally.

485
00:37:21,261 --> 00:37:23,845
Scott: I noticed this.

486
00:37:23,865 --> 00:37:31,439
Scott: During every combat scene, they change the coloring and the line style to be like this combat...

487
00:37:33,140 --> 00:37:35,110
Rym: Yeah, with this purple highlight.

488
00:37:35,311 --> 00:37:37,140
Rym: You'll notice it when you watch the show.

489
00:37:37,980 --> 00:37:49,840
Scott: And there are other times where... I couldn't find a pattern for this style, but there are other times where the style changes to have screen tones and dots, like a manga all over.

490
00:37:50,181 --> 00:37:55,520
Scott: I couldn't find a pattern of when they were using that style, but it kept happening.

491
00:37:56,320 --> 00:38:02,820
Scott: So it's like most of the show is sort of in this, quote, "default anime style," I guess you want to call it, whatever.

492
00:38:03,400 --> 00:38:16,520
Scott: But if you pay attention, you'll see, hey, they keep dramatically changing the visual style of the show, depending on the scene, according to some pattern of some kind, some logic.

493
00:38:16,640 --> 00:38:31,800
Scott: I don't know, but I've seen shows change their style before, like suddenly everyone's chibi because someone's getting bopped on the head or something, like in Slayers, or suddenly everyone's serious face with extra high detail.

494
00:38:32,460 --> 00:38:39,420
Scott: But it's like this show is doing that same effect of style changing, but not in the same way.

495
00:38:39,580 --> 00:38:47,700
Scott: It's changing the coloring and the lines and that sort of thing, as opposed to changing the detail level or anything else.

496
00:38:47,780 --> 00:38:48,923
Rym: And there's just a lot of craft to it.

497
00:38:48,963 --> 00:38:51,088
Rym: Like, it's visually really good.

498
00:38:51,188 --> 00:38:53,494
Rym: I'd say it's above average for an anime.

499
00:38:53,514 --> 00:38:54,376
Rym: It's got a good budget.

500
00:38:55,258 --> 00:38:55,880
Rym: It looks great.

501
00:38:56,420 --> 00:39:04,080
Rym: And it also, the original show does have a lot of animation shortcuts because it ran forever and had a limited amount of money.

502
00:39:05,021 --> 00:39:16,160
Rym: This one really uses the fact that it is animated and there's a lot of visual style and effects and it makes the show just a lot punchier.

503
00:39:16,360 --> 00:39:18,448
Rym: And honestly, I think a lot funnier.

504
00:39:18,890 --> 00:39:21,119
Rym: I think it is funnier than the original so far.

505
00:39:22,561 --> 00:39:26,046
Scott: It depends if you like the kind of humor that it is, right?

506
00:39:26,126 --> 00:39:33,017
Scott: It's a lot of very sort of juvenile, but it's not the filthy juvenile.

507
00:39:35,660 --> 00:39:52,340
Rym: I would say it is heartfelt juvenile comedy in that it is juvenile, but the joke where he notices her panties is done in what I would consider a juvenile but tasteful and kind of wholesome, funny way.

508
00:39:52,800 --> 00:40:00,180
Rym: As opposed to, say, a lecherous anime where it would be used more for both a juvenile and prurient way.

509
00:40:01,145 --> 00:40:03,820
Scott: Right, but there's a lot of slapstick nonsense going on.

510
00:40:03,820 --> 00:40:05,420
Rym: Yeah, I like slapstick nonsense in anime.

511
00:40:06,761 --> 00:40:11,632
Scott: Yeah, but it's not quite the Looney Tunes kind of slapstick, right?

512
00:40:11,752 --> 00:40:15,060
Scott: It's more, you know, I guess it's the anime style.

513
00:40:17,801 --> 00:40:26,837
Rym: So, I would say if you love the old Ranma and haven't thought about Ranma since the 90s, watch a couple episodes of this.

514
00:40:28,600 --> 00:40:30,319
Scott: This is going to take you back to the past.

515
00:40:30,660 --> 00:40:42,280
Rym: Yep, and I just want to keep watching it like I'm actually really enjoying watching it in a way that I'm sort of almost experiencing Ranma properly for the first time because I never watched it in order.

516
00:40:42,580 --> 00:40:51,056
Rym: I read random volumes sitting in Borders books and I watched random episodes of this and whatever VHS tapes I happen to borrow from someone.

517
00:40:51,336 --> 00:40:53,360
Rym: I've never seen this story like in order.

518
00:40:55,041 --> 00:40:59,306
Rym: If you've never experienced a Ranma thing, I'd also say watch a couple episodes.

519
00:40:59,647 --> 00:41:09,520
Rym: If nothing else, you'll see the kind of anime that was crucial and deeply rooted in the early days of American anime fandom.

520
00:41:10,120 --> 00:41:17,580
Rym: Like, this is one of the shows that left its long stain on how American anime fans engage with anime.

521
00:41:19,063 --> 00:41:21,800
Rym: And if you really like it and you never read the manga, the manga's worth reading.

522
00:41:26,040 --> 00:41:36,460
Scott: I'd say, I'm not going to say this is the best thing ever, you've got to go watch it, but I'm going to say everyone should at least, if you're an anime fan, not a normal person.

523
00:41:37,120 --> 00:41:42,013
Scott: But if you're an anime fan, if you've got Netflix, don't go buy Netflix to watch this.

524
00:41:42,996 --> 00:41:44,339
Scott: Or if you're going to pirate it, that's cool too.

525
00:41:45,161 --> 00:41:54,280
Scott: You know, watch some and at the very minimum, you should just watch some because you should know what the deal is with some famous anime of all time.

526
00:41:54,860 --> 00:42:01,059
Scott: And this new version is probably the best way to understand what the deal is with this famous show.

527
00:42:02,701 --> 00:42:13,200
Scott: And then if you watch enough so that you know what the deal is, then you can decide for yourself if you want to go hard or just be satisfied with the amount you've seen.

528
00:42:18,082 --> 00:42:20,470
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

529
00:42:20,511 --> 00:42:23,040
Rym: Special thanks to DJ Pretzel for the opening theme.

530
00:42:23,240 --> 00:42:29,260
Scott: Be sure to visit our website at FrontRowClue.com for show notes, discussion, links and more.

531
